\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces One passage, several possible pairs. Retrieval proposes a candidate when a passage names two organisms near an interaction word (bold). The sentence question is asked of the passage, so it has one answer whichever pair the candidate names: even a perfect answer accepts the third pair, two bacterial genera merely listed together. Only the third pair is a benchmark item (\textsc  {Test299}, expert-graded negative); the sentence-level model accepts it and the pair- and triple-conditioned models reject it at their block-held-out thresholds. The first two pairs are illustrative. Bottom: model inputs.\relax }}{1}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:concept}{{1}{1}{One passage, several possible pairs. Retrieval proposes a candidate when a passage names two organisms near an interaction word (bold). The sentence question is asked of the passage, so it has one answer whichever pair the candidate names: even a perfect answer accepts the third pair, two bacterial genera merely listed together. Only the third pair is a benchmark item (\textsc {Test299}, expert-graded negative); the sentence-level model accepts it and the pair- and triple-conditioned models reject it at their block-held-out thresholds. The first two pairs are illustrative. Bottom: model inputs.\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
