\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The controlled comparison: one encoder, one training corpus per benchmark, three seeds; only the input differs. AUPRC ($\pm $ standard deviation) and EP F1 (BioRED's document-level entity-pair F1) are per-seed means; F1 is that of three-checkpoint ensembles at block-held-out thresholds (biodiversity) or at the pre-specified threshold (BioRED). BioRED has no relation term, hence no triple-conditioned model. Accepting every candidate gives AUPRC $0.574$ and F1 $0.730$ on our benchmark; BioRED's base rate is $0.463$. Against the sentence-level model, the pair-conditioned one fixes 74 items and breaks 23 (McNemar $p = 3.8\times 10^{-7}$), and 2{,}642 and 1{,}064 on BioRED ($p = 5.9\times 10^{-148}$); the triple-conditioned one fixes 74 and breaks 32 ($p = 6.8\times 10^{-5}$), and against the pair-conditioned one 21 and 30 ($p = 0.26$). Per-seed values (biodiversity): Appendix~\ref  {app:seeds}.\relax }}{5}{table.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{1}{5}{The controlled comparison: one encoder, one training corpus per benchmark, three seeds; only the input differs. AUPRC ($\pm $ standard deviation) and EP F1 (BioRED's document-level entity-pair F1) are per-seed means; F1 is that of three-checkpoint ensembles at block-held-out thresholds (biodiversity) or at the pre-specified threshold (BioRED). BioRED has no relation term, hence no triple-conditioned model. Accepting every candidate gives AUPRC $0.574$ and F1 $0.730$ on our benchmark; BioRED's base rate is $0.463$. Against the sentence-level model, the pair-conditioned one fixes 74 items and breaks 23 (McNemar $p = 3.8\times 10^{-7}$), and 2{,}642 and 1{,}064 on BioRED ($p = 5.9\times 10^{-148}$); the triple-conditioned one fixes 74 and breaks 32 ($p = 6.8\times 10^{-5}$), and against the pair-conditioned one 21 and 30 ($p = 0.26$). Per-seed values (biodiversity): Appendix~\ref {app:seeds}.\relax }{table.caption.10}{}}
