\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The same gap in zero-shot language models: AUPRC of Qwen models \citep  {qwen2025technical} asked the sentence question (dashed, open squares) or the pair question (solid, filled circles) on (a)~the 437 expert-graded candidates and (b)~a 3{,}000-candidate BioRED sample. Dotted: the trained 110M models on the same rows (three-checkpoint ensembles); grey: base rate. From 1.7B on, the pair question leads at each size on both benchmarks, while the sentence question gains little with scale. $^*$Mixture of experts, placed by total size; the 32B model is the teacher. $^\dagger $Qwen3.5-122B, a later release, ran on the first 1{,}000 BioRED candidates (base rate $0.442$; trained models $0.714$ and $0.841$). Asked the triple question (our benchmark only), the models track or exceed the pair question (Appendix~\ref  {app:scaling}).\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{4}{6}{The same gap in zero-shot language models: AUPRC of Qwen models \citep {qwen2025technical} asked the sentence question (dashed, open squares) or the pair question (solid, filled circles) on (a)~the 437 expert-graded candidates and (b)~a 3{,}000-candidate BioRED sample. Dotted: the trained 110M models on the same rows (three-checkpoint ensembles); grey: base rate. From 1.7B on, the pair question leads at each size on both benchmarks, while the sentence question gains little with scale. $^*$Mixture of experts, placed by total size; the 32B model is the teacher. $^\dagger $Qwen3.5-122B, a later release, ran on the first 1{,}000 BioRED candidates (base rate $0.442$; trained models $0.714$ and $0.841$). Asked the triple question (our benchmark only), the models track or exceed the pair question (Appendix~\ref {app:scaling}).\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
