\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The sentence-level, pair-conditioned and triple-conditioned models trained on the teacher's verdicts (the body's models) and on its probabilities, recipe and seeds unchanged. AUPRC is the per-seed mean $\pm $ standard deviation; F1 (block-held-out thresholds) and AUPRC by the number of taxa the passage names are those of three-checkpoint ensembles, as in Tables~\ref  {tab:main} and~\ref  {tab:where}. Per-seed AUPRC with soft targets: $.866/.879/.875$, $.951/.946/.945$ and $.952/.952/.948$. Against the verdict-trained model of the same format, the soft-target model fixes 13 items and breaks 7 (sentence-level), 13 and 14 (pair-conditioned), 19 and 7 (triple-conditioned; McNemar $p = 0.26$, $1.0$, $0.03$).\relax }}{15}{table.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:soft}{{7}{15}{The sentence-level, pair-conditioned and triple-conditioned models trained on the teacher's verdicts (the body's models) and on its probabilities, recipe and seeds unchanged. AUPRC is the per-seed mean $\pm $ standard deviation; F1 (block-held-out thresholds) and AUPRC by the number of taxa the passage names are those of three-checkpoint ensembles, as in Tables~\ref {tab:main} and~\ref {tab:where}. Per-seed AUPRC with soft targets: $.866/.879/.875$, $.951/.946/.945$ and $.952/.952/.948$. Against the verdict-trained model of the same format, the soft-target model fixes 13 items and breaks 7 (sentence-level), 13 and 14 (pair-conditioned), 19 and 7 (triple-conditioned; McNemar $p = 0.26$, $1.0$, $0.03$).\relax }{table.caption.41}{}}
